At four in the morning, in a beech wood in the west of England, Tom Arkwright sits on a folding stool with headphones over his ears and does not move for two hours. Beside him, a pair of microphones on a low stand point into the darkness. He is waiting for the dawn chorus, as he has done on some four hundred mornings over the past decade.

{{41}}

The change is already audible to anyone with a point of comparison. Arkwright plays me two recordings made in the same clearing on the same date, eleven years apart. The first is dense, almost chaotic, with a dozen species competing to be heard. The second is recognisably the same place, but thinner, with long gaps in which nothing sings at all.

{{42}}

Those others have begun to take an interest. Two years ago, a team of ecologists approached him with a proposal. Software now exists that can identify individual species in a recording and count them far faster than any human listener. His archive, made with the same equipment in the same locations year after year, was exactly the kind of consistent record they had lacked.

{{43}}

That reason is hard for him to put into words, and he is visibly uneasy when asked to try. He talks about attention, about the way an hour of listening changes what you are able to hear. Sounds that were a blur on the first morning separate, by the fortieth, into individuals with habits and territories.

{{44}}

It would be easy to present Arkwright as a man recording an elegy, and he resists the description firmly. Some of his sites, he insists, sound better than they did. A stretch of river where the banks have been left to grow wild now has species he never heard there before.

{{45}}

By half past six the chorus has faded into ordinary daytime birdsong, and Arkwright begins to pack up. He labels the recording with the date and the temperature, as he always does, and adds a note that a tractor started up at ten past five.

{{46}}
